win32u: a window that cannot be closed from a button it does not have.

The pair to 0068, and it exists because that patch got two buttons out of
three. Masking WS_MINIMIZEBOX and WS_MAXIMIZEBOX out of what a program reads
removed minimise and maximise from VS Code and left close exactly where it was,
because Electron does not ask the style whether it may close:
NativeWindowViews::IsClosable calls GetSystemMenu and reads the MFS_DISABLED
bit on SC_CLOSE.

SC_CLOSE is greyed rather than the whole system menu removed. Returning no menu
was tried first and is worse for no gain: both make IsClosable answer no, but
no menu takes the Alt+Space window menu with it, and Move and Size on that menu
are how a window is placed when there is no caption left to drag.

Closing is untouched. WM_CLOSE and WM_SYSCOMMAND SC_CLOSE are handled by
DefWindowProc, which never reads this state, and the task bar sends its close
that way -- the division 0010 records for minimise and force-close. What a user
loses is a greyed Close row on a menu they reach with Alt+Space, beside Move and
Size rows that still work.

Top-level windows only: WS_SYSMENU on a child means something else entirely.

This does not reach a program that draws its buttons without asking. Pale Moon
is one -- Gecko paints its own title bar unconditionally -- and is handled on
the Vessel side instead, by a default preference shipped with the browser.

--- a/dlls/win32u/menu.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/menu.c
@@ -1589,6 +1589,7 @@
     HMENU retvalue = 0;
 
     if (win == WND_DESKTOP || !win) return 0;
+
     if (win == WND_OTHER_PROCESS)
     {
         if (is_window( hwnd )) FIXME( "not supported on other process window %p\n", hwnd );
@@ -1602,8 +1603,35 @@
     }
 
     if (!win->hSysMenu && (win->dwStyle & WS_SYSMENU))
+    {
         win->hSysMenu = get_sys_menu( hwnd, 0 );
 
+        /* Vessel: a window that cannot be closed from a button it does not have.
+         *
+         * The pair to the style mask in patches/wine/0068, and it exists because
+         * that patch got two buttons out of three. Electron does not ask the
+         * style whether it may close: NativeWindowViews::IsClosable calls
+         * GetSystemMenu and reads the MFS_DISABLED bit on SC_CLOSE, so masking
+         * WS_SYSMENU out of what a program reads left the close button exactly
+         * where it was while minimise and maximise disappeared.
+         *
+         * Greyed rather than the whole menu removed, which was tried first and
+         * is worse for no gain: both make IsClosable answer no, but returning no
+         * menu at all takes the Alt+Space window menu with it, and Move and Size
+         * on that menu are how a window is placed without a caption to drag.
+         *
+         * Closing is untouched. WM_CLOSE and WM_SYSCOMMAND SC_CLOSE are handled
+         * by DefWindowProc, which never reads this state, and the task bar sends
+         * its close that way -- the division 0010 records for minimise and
+         * force-close. What a user loses is the greyed Close row on a menu they
+         * reach with Alt+Space, next to the Move and Size rows that still work.
+         *
+         * Top-level only: a child window has no caption and no buttons, and
+         * WS_SYSMENU on one means something else entirely. */
+        if (win->hSysMenu && !(win->dwStyle & WS_CHILD))
+            NtUserEnableMenuItem( win->hSysMenu, SC_CLOSE, MF_BYCOMMAND | MF_GRAYED );
+    }
+
     if (win->hSysMenu)
     {
         struct menu *menu;
